WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:01.516 --> 00:07.416 align:middle
Transcript of Chairman Warsh's
Press Conference June 17,

00:07.546 --> 00:09.616 align:middle
2026 CHAIRMAN WARSH.

00:10.276 --> 00:12.556 align:middle
Good day. It's an
honor, a true honor,

00:12.556 --> 00:16.226 align:middle
to be back at the Federal
Reserve and to take up this duty

00:16.226 --> 00:18.786 align:middle
at a time of such consequence.

00:20.026 --> 00:23.106 align:middle
I've been especially
heartened by the warm welcome

00:23.106 --> 00:25.906 align:middle
of old friends and
new colleagues, both,

00:27.126 --> 00:30.626 align:middle
and I've listened closely
to my fellow FOMC members.

00:31.096 --> 00:33.686 align:middle
I've heard a lot of new
ideas, new thinking,

00:34.496 --> 00:37.226 align:middle
and genuine interest in
moving the Fed forward.

00:38.396 --> 00:42.956 align:middle
This week's FOMC meeting
exemplified the very best

00:42.956 --> 00:45.486 align:middle
of the Fed's traditions:
rigorous debate,

00:46.586 --> 00:51.626 align:middle
open-mindedness, commitment
to mission, responsibility,

00:52.466 --> 00:54.866 align:middle
and accountability
for performance.

00:55.846 --> 00:58.936 align:middle
In this business, they
all add up to one thing:

00:59.746 --> 01:05.156 align:middle
getting monetary policy right-or
as near to it as we can do.

01:06.176 --> 01:07.666 align:middle
That is our North Star.

01:08.916 --> 01:12.436 align:middle
My colleagues and I are here
to serve our legislative remit,

01:13.286 --> 01:16.086 align:middle
which you've heard us say
before-price stability

01:16.506 --> 01:21.746 align:middle
and maximum employment-and these
objectives guided our business

01:22.106 --> 01:23.306 align:middle
in the meeting just concluded.

01:24.806 --> 01:27.846 align:middle
As you saw a few moments
ago, the Committee decided

01:27.846 --> 01:31.916 align:middle
to maintain the target range
for the fed funds rate at 3½

01:32.316 --> 01:36.996 align:middle
to 3  percent, in support
of the Fed's dual mandate.

01:38.646 --> 01:41.416 align:middle
The Committee also
reaffirmed its policy

01:41.416 --> 01:44.356 align:middle
of maintaining ample reserves
in the banking system.

01:45.806 --> 01:47.986 align:middle
Economic activity is expanding

01:47.986 --> 01:52.466 align:middle
at a solid pace despite elevated
uncertainty that owes, in part,

01:52.466 --> 01:54.296 align:middle
to the conflict in
the Middle East.

01:55.636 --> 01:59.536 align:middle
Productivity growth and
capital investment-both strong.

02:00.656 --> 02:03.016 align:middle
Job gains have kept
pace with the workforce,

02:03.406 --> 02:05.626 align:middle
and the unemployment
rate has changed little.

02:07.086 --> 02:10.916 align:middle
We recognize that inflation
has been running well ahead

02:11.486 --> 02:13.846 align:middle
of the Fed's long-stated
inflation goal

02:13.846 --> 02:17.566 align:middle
of 2 percent that's been going
on for more than five years.

02:19.746 --> 02:23.806 align:middle
Persistently high prices are a
burden for the American people.

02:25.256 --> 02:27.786 align:middle
But the recent past
need not be prologue.

02:29.056 --> 02:31.976 align:middle
I am pleased to report
that members

02:31.976 --> 02:36.416 align:middle
of the FOMC are unambiguous
and unanimous:

02:36.836 --> 02:39.886 align:middle
This Committee will
deliver price stability.

02:41.306 --> 02:47.396 align:middle
At any institution, a change
in leadership is a natural

02:47.396 --> 02:50.896 align:middle
and timely opportunity
to reaffirm its mission,

02:52.066 --> 02:54.066 align:middle
to review current practices,

02:54.756 --> 02:57.796 align:middle
and to consider whether
those practices best meet

02:57.796 --> 02:58.706 align:middle
our objectives.

02:59.586 --> 03:03.186 align:middle
My Fed colleagues and I will be
working in close collaboration

03:03.736 --> 03:07.116 align:middle
to ask what changes
might improve the conduct

03:07.116 --> 03:08.096 align:middle
of monetary policy.

03:09.456 --> 03:12.226 align:middle
On that score, you might have
already noticed something:

03:12.786 --> 03:14.896 align:middle
a difference in today's
policy statement.

03:15.736 --> 03:19.306 align:middle
It's a bit shorter, a bit
simpler-and it dispenses

03:19.306 --> 03:20.486 align:middle
with some older language.

03:21.606 --> 03:23.526 align:middle
That statement just
gives you the facts,

03:23.676 --> 03:24.866 align:middle
as best we can judge it.

03:25.946 --> 03:30.386 align:middle
Absent, also, is so-called
forward guidance-which we agreed

03:30.386 --> 03:33.346 align:middle
was not well suited to the
current policy conjuncture.

03:34.776 --> 03:38.556 align:middle
This afternoon, you also
received the usual Summary

03:38.556 --> 03:39.966 align:middle
of Economic Projections.

03:40.946 --> 03:44.106 align:middle
It's been the practice of this
Committee for participants

03:44.136 --> 03:45.596 align:middle
to submit these projections,

03:46.056 --> 03:49.106 align:middle
and I have encouraged my
colleagues to continue to do so.

03:50.346 --> 03:53.226 align:middle
I, however, have refrained
from offering any projections

03:53.226 --> 03:56.846 align:middle
of my own, consistent with my
long-held views on the SEP,

03:56.846 --> 03:59.276 align:middle
at least as currently
structured.

04:00.666 --> 04:03.356 align:middle
In the median projections,
real GDP rises

04:03.356 --> 04:07.636 align:middle
at 2.2 percent this year,
2.3 percent next year,

04:08.386 --> 04:12.686 align:middle
and total PCE inflation runs
at 3.6 percent this year,

04:13.336 --> 04:14.886 align:middle
2.3 percent next year.

04:15.596 --> 04:18.696 align:middle
The unemployment rate
stands at about 4.3 percent.

04:19.686 --> 04:21.536 align:middle
The median participant judges

04:21.536 --> 04:25.356 align:middle
that the appropriate federal
funds rate to be at 3.8 percent

04:25.356 --> 04:29.256 align:middle
at the end of this year
and 3.6 at the end of next.

04:30.236 --> 04:33.576 align:middle
Let me turn now to a few
words on a key initiative

04:34.156 --> 04:35.226 align:middle
that we're announcing today.

04:36.776 --> 04:39.916 align:middle
I'm appointing a task
force in each of five areas

04:40.336 --> 04:43.466 align:middle
that are central to the broad
conduct of monetary policy:

04:44.336 --> 04:49.446 align:middle
First, Fed communications;
second, the Fed's balance sheet;

04:50.536 --> 04:56.786 align:middle
third, our use and reliance on
existing data sources; fourth,

04:57.306 --> 05:01.826 align:middle
productivity and jobs in an era
of transformation; and last,

05:01.996 --> 05:03.646 align:middle
the Fed's inflation frameworks.

05:04.466 --> 05:10.036 align:middle
These subjects are timely,
consequential, and, in my view,

05:10.036 --> 05:11.336 align:middle
worthy of a fresh look.

05:12.446 --> 05:15.816 align:middle
My colleagues and I discussed
them with energy and purpose

05:16.076 --> 05:17.486 align:middle
over the last couple of days.

05:18.636 --> 05:22.396 align:middle
For each of these independent
task forces, I'm enlisting some

05:22.396 --> 05:25.206 align:middle
of the very best
minds-both inside

05:25.206 --> 05:26.986 align:middle
and outside the economics
profession.

05:28.196 --> 05:30.946 align:middle
They will be supported by
subject matter specialists

05:30.946 --> 05:32.356 align:middle
from our superb Fed staff.

05:33.606 --> 05:35.436 align:middle
And they will have a
straightforward charge:

05:35.936 --> 05:39.856 align:middle
Start with first principles;
ask hard questions;

05:40.676 --> 05:45.476 align:middle
examine current practice;
consider alternatives; and,

05:45.476 --> 05:49.206 align:middle
ultimately, propose next steps
for policymaker consideration.

05:50.486 --> 05:54.626 align:middle
Since last summer, my colleagues
discussed possible improvements

05:54.626 --> 05:57.256 align:middle
in the form and function
of Fed communications.

05:58.216 --> 06:02.546 align:middle
This new task force will build
on that effort and, I expect,

06:03.076 --> 06:05.316 align:middle
propose some well-considered
changes,

06:05.726 --> 06:08.406 align:middle
including to the SEP I
mentioned a few moments ago.

06:09.616 --> 06:12.696 align:middle
The second task force, the
one on balance sheet policy,

06:13.356 --> 06:14.986 align:middle
will review the benefits
and risks

06:14.986 --> 06:19.096 align:middle
of the current ample-reserves
regime and the composition

06:19.316 --> 06:20.366 align:middle
of the Fed's balance sheet.

06:21.536 --> 06:24.276 align:middle
They will assess alternative
frameworks for the conduct

06:24.746 --> 06:26.846 align:middle
and operation of
monetary policy.

06:28.246 --> 06:30.516 align:middle
The third task force,
the one on data,

06:31.276 --> 06:33.506 align:middle
will evaluate new
information sources

06:33.946 --> 06:36.526 align:middle
and consider methodological
changes

06:36.526 --> 06:39.136 align:middle
to improve data gathering,
with the aim

06:39.136 --> 06:42.796 align:middle
of giving policymakers
more accurate, relevant,

06:43.286 --> 06:46.636 align:middle
contemporaneous, and,
perhaps most important,

06:46.856 --> 06:49.686 align:middle
actionable information on
the state of our economy.

06:50.536 --> 06:53.976 align:middle
Fourth, the task force
on productivity and jobs.

06:54.626 --> 06:58.736 align:middle
It'll survey the pace, the
reach, the economic impact

06:59.676 --> 07:02.956 align:middle
of new general-purpose
technologies, including AI,

07:02.956 --> 07:08.546 align:middle
and explore the implications
for the Fed in pursuit

07:08.546 --> 07:10.806 align:middle
of our employment and
inflation mandates.

07:11.426 --> 07:14.646 align:middle
The last task force, the
one on inflation frameworks,

07:15.446 --> 07:18.906 align:middle
that'll examine the drivers of
inflation, first principles,

07:19.306 --> 07:21.266 align:middle
and weigh the full
range of ideas

07:21.566 --> 07:24.576 align:middle
for delivering price stability
in a changing economy.

07:25.946 --> 07:29.146 align:middle
You'll hear quite a bit
more about these task forces

07:29.146 --> 07:31.406 align:middle
and this overall initiative
in the coming weeks.

07:32.176 --> 07:36.436 align:middle
Enough for now to make a simple
statement-each task force will

07:36.436 --> 07:39.446 align:middle
serve an objective shared
by everyone in the System,

07:40.106 --> 07:42.316 align:middle
shared by everyone around
that table that I sat

07:42.316 --> 07:45.516 align:middle
with over the last couple
of days: a Federal Reserve

07:45.516 --> 07:49.716 align:middle
that is clear eyed about its
mission, fit for purpose,

07:50.016 --> 07:51.106 align:middle
and focused on the future.

07:52.056 --> 07:54.206 align:middle
And with that, I
appreciate your attention.

07:54.356 --> 07:55.856 align:middle
I'm happy to take
your questions.

07:56.416 --> 07:57.096 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

07:57.096 --> 07:57.936 align:middle
Howard Schneider.

07:57.936 --> 07:58.976 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

08:00.446 --> 08:01.206 align:middle
Hi, Chairman.

08:01.206 --> 08:02.006 align:middle
Howard Schneider, with Reuters.

08:02.006 --> 08:04.576 align:middle
Good to see you again,
and, and, and welcome back.

08:04.836 --> 08:07.446 align:middle
This is a lot to be
putting in motion so fast.

08:07.936 --> 08:11.496 align:middle
What is the timeline you have
in mind for, for each of these?

08:12.296 --> 08:13.186 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

08:13.186 --> 08:17.026 align:middle
So I think it'll depend on the
task force and also depends

08:17.026 --> 08:20.076 align:middle
on the urgency in which
we need clear answers.

08:20.666 --> 08:24.056 align:middle
My expectation-I'm still in
the business of recruiting

08:24.056 --> 08:27.596 align:middle
and finalizing them-my
expectation is the task forces

08:27.596 --> 08:31.186 align:middle
will begin work in the next
couple of weeks, and we'll start

08:31.186 --> 08:34.526 align:middle
to get some more information
from them, some more framing

08:34.526 --> 08:38.576 align:middle
of how they see things starting
in the fall, and hopefully most,

08:38.576 --> 08:40.546 align:middle
if not all of them,
concluding by year-end.

08:40.666 --> 08:41.296 align:middle
HOWARD SCHNEIDER.

08:41.296 --> 08:44.446 align:middle
And just specifically on
the inflation framework,

08:44.446 --> 08:48.256 align:middle
you talk about first principles,
does this include a review

08:48.636 --> 08:50.966 align:middle
of the 2 percent target itself?

08:50.966 --> 08:52.566 align:middle
You've mentioned that
things to the right

08:52.566 --> 08:54.076 align:middle
of the decimal point
don't matter.

08:54.756 --> 08:58.636 align:middle
Should this be starting from
a premise that 2 percent

08:58.696 --> 09:00.596 align:middle
as a point estimate
is, is too strict?

09:00.596 --> 09:01.136 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

09:01.136 --> 09:03.136 align:middle
Let me break that
into two pieces.

09:03.336 --> 09:08.566 align:middle
First on the inflation framework
review, their remit is,

09:08.696 --> 09:10.436 align:middle
what are the drivers
of inflation?

09:10.616 --> 09:13.036 align:middle
What's the Fed's
responsibility for inflation?

09:13.356 --> 09:15.376 align:middle
In part, how do we
measure inflation?

09:15.376 --> 09:17.326 align:middle
But that'll overlap
with my data group.

09:17.886 --> 09:20.276 align:middle
On the 2 percent
inflation objective,

09:20.466 --> 09:23.706 align:middle
that is the Federal
Reserve's long-held objective

09:23.706 --> 09:24.446 align:middle
of 2 percent.

09:25.216 --> 09:28.096 align:middle
You've heard me say
before, I tend to focus

09:28.096 --> 09:29.446 align:middle
on the left of the
decimal point.

09:29.816 --> 09:31.666 align:middle
Well, the two is the left
of the decimal point.

09:31.666 --> 09:33.146 align:middle
For now, zero is to the right.

09:33.556 --> 09:38.206 align:middle
I see no reason until we have
reestablished our commitment

09:38.206 --> 09:41.796 align:middle
and ability to deliver on the
2 percent inflation objective

09:41.796 --> 09:42.686 align:middle
to revisit that.

09:42.756 --> 09:45.216 align:middle
So that'll be outside the
scope of what we're taking on.

09:45.506 --> 09:45.816 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

09:47.576 --> 09:49.726 align:middle
Colby. COLBY SMITH.

09:50.236 --> 09:50.946 align:middle
Thank you so much.

09:50.946 --> 09:52.286 align:middle
Colby Smith with
the New York Times.

09:52.736 --> 09:54.906 align:middle
You've, in the past, said
that inflation is a choice,

09:54.976 --> 09:57.766 align:middle
and in the policy statement,
it includes this pledge

09:57.856 --> 10:00.036 align:middle
to deliver price stability,
as you've reiterated today.

10:00.826 --> 10:02.306 align:middle
But looking at the SEP, the bulk

10:02.306 --> 10:05.766 align:middle
of your colleagues expect core
PCE to run around 3.3 percent

10:05.766 --> 10:09.676 align:middle
by year-end and for the 2
percent inflation target not

10:09.676 --> 10:11.426 align:middle
to be reached until 2028.

10:12.086 --> 10:15.186 align:middle
So I'm curious how patient you
think the Fed can afford to be

10:15.366 --> 10:17.216 align:middle
at this juncture
in terms of waiting

10:17.216 --> 10:20.546 align:middle
for one-time inflation
waves to wash through

10:21.046 --> 10:24.466 align:middle
and for underlying inflation to
step down after so many years

10:24.506 --> 10:26.696 align:middle
of inflation running
above target?

10:26.696 --> 10:30.306 align:middle
And under what circumstances
you would support the Fed taking

10:30.356 --> 10:31.676 align:middle
some action and raising rates?

10:31.946 --> 10:32.316 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

10:32.316 --> 10:33.996 align:middle
Sure. So quite a bit there.

10:33.996 --> 10:35.896 align:middle
Let me-let me try to
break that into pieces.

10:36.656 --> 10:40.686 align:middle
First, we have the capability
and commitment to deliver

10:40.946 --> 10:43.186 align:middle
on our price-stability
objective of 2 percent.

10:43.466 --> 10:46.896 align:middle
That's exactly what
we're going to do.

10:46.896 --> 10:49.436 align:middle
In the Fed's review
of its strategy

10:49.436 --> 10:52.076 align:middle
over the last any number
of years-in January,

10:52.296 --> 10:54.386 align:middle
the Fed-including the strategy

10:54.386 --> 10:57.506 align:middle
that we're still bound
by-the Fed statement says

10:57.506 --> 11:00.906 align:middle
that inflation is primarily
determined by monetary policy.

11:01.176 --> 11:02.016 align:middle
You bet it is.

11:02.586 --> 11:05.626 align:middle
I've said for years
inflation is a choice.

11:05.726 --> 11:06.586 align:middle
You bet it is.

11:07.066 --> 11:10.086 align:middle
And today I'm announcing that
this Committee, unambiguously

11:10.086 --> 11:13.726 align:middle
and unanimously, have decided
we are going to deliver on that.

11:14.386 --> 11:17.466 align:middle
Rest of your question sounded
like an encouragement for me

11:17.466 --> 11:18.666 align:middle
to give forward guidance.

11:19.376 --> 11:20.836 align:middle
We've dropped forward guidance.

11:21.316 --> 11:24.406 align:middle
Some along the Committee
I think dropped it,

11:24.406 --> 11:26.906 align:middle
I suspect from our discussion
the last couple of days,

11:27.126 --> 11:30.396 align:middle
because they said, at this
moment in time, it doesn't feel

11:30.396 --> 11:32.376 align:middle
as though providing
forward guidance is right.

11:32.986 --> 11:35.456 align:middle
Others have, I'd say,
different views and think

11:35.456 --> 11:39.266 align:middle
as a general proposition forward
guidance isn't the business we

11:39.266 --> 11:40.046 align:middle
should be in.

11:40.496 --> 11:43.626 align:middle
But that'll be taken up by the
task force on communications,

11:43.626 --> 11:47.876 align:middle
and my policymaker colleagues,
we're going to listen hard

11:47.876 --> 11:49.906 align:middle
to what the experts say
and make our own decision.

11:50.426 --> 11:52.566 align:middle
But I can't give
any forward guidance

11:52.566 --> 11:53.676 align:middle
about what we're
going to do next.

11:53.676 --> 11:56.456 align:middle
The good news is, we'll
be meeting in six weeks.

11:56.456 --> 11:56.956 align:middle
COLBY SMITH.

11:57.016 --> 11:58.036 align:middle
So just following up, I guess

11:58.066 --> 11:59.726 align:middle
on the current policy
settings then,

11:59.986 --> 12:03.096 align:middle
I am curious how restrictive
you think things are

12:03.096 --> 12:05.986 align:middle
at the current moment,
given the flow of data

12:05.986 --> 12:07.696 align:middle
that we've seen and, you know,

12:07.756 --> 12:09.576 align:middle
forecasts that are
coming down the pipeline.

12:09.866 --> 12:10.376 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

12:10.376 --> 12:12.866 align:middle
Yeah. I-I've heard
characterizations,

12:12.866 --> 12:14.636 align:middle
both inside in the
Fed about that.

12:14.636 --> 12:15.726 align:middle
I'll give you my own.

12:16.306 --> 12:17.116 align:middle
It's uneven.

12:17.976 --> 12:20.736 align:middle
And, if I look at the housing
markets as one example,

12:21.356 --> 12:25.306 align:middle
Fed policy isn't the, the
single determinant of the state

12:25.306 --> 12:28.276 align:middle
of the housing market, but
broadly I would say, there,

12:28.276 --> 12:31.286 align:middle
Fed policy appears to
be somewhat restrictive.

12:32.266 --> 12:35.976 align:middle
I would have a hard time
managing to say those words

12:35.976 --> 12:39.016 align:middle
if I were to see what's
happening in financial markets,

12:39.016 --> 12:40.106 align:middle
so I'd say it's uneven.

12:40.806 --> 12:42.186 align:middle
That's perhaps a function

12:42.186 --> 12:45.576 align:middle
of different transmission
mechanisms of monetary policy,

12:45.886 --> 12:47.706 align:middle
whether monetary
policy is coming

12:47.706 --> 12:50.046 align:middle
from our interest rate tool
or our balance sheet tool.

12:50.046 --> 12:52.616 align:middle
But the good news, we have
a task force on that, too,

12:52.616 --> 12:54.446 align:middle
and the balance sheet task
force will be looking more

12:54.446 --> 12:55.036 align:middle
at that subject.

12:55.036 --> 12:55.476 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

12:56.476 --> 12:56.756 align:middle
Mike McKee.

12:56.756 --> 12:59.476 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

12:59.476 --> 13:01.806 align:middle
You've said you, you don't
like forward guidance.

13:01.806 --> 13:03.996 align:middle
You dropped it from the
statement this time.

13:03.996 --> 13:06.926 align:middle
But with the dot plot,
nine members suggested

13:06.926 --> 13:09.956 align:middle
that they want a rate increase
by the end of the year,

13:10.366 --> 13:13.546 align:middle
and the markets have taken
that as forward guidance.

13:13.546 --> 13:17.986 align:middle
So what does this mean in terms
of how you guide the markets

13:18.036 --> 13:23.266 align:middle
and in terms of what the
dot plot's future is?

13:23.266 --> 13:24.876 align:middle
CHARIMAN WARSH.

13:24.876 --> 13:27.706 align:middle
I'm going to have to give you
the same answer I gave to,

13:27.706 --> 13:30.196 align:middle
to Ms. Smith; we've got
a task force for that.

13:30.336 --> 13:33.616 align:middle
I'll give you a little bit more.

13:34.356 --> 13:38.206 align:middle
I reviewed the dot plots, and
when I saw the submissions,

13:38.306 --> 13:41.406 align:middle
I noted that all the submissions
were coming in with pencils,

13:41.806 --> 13:43.526 align:middle
you know those kinds
with the big erasers.

13:44.796 --> 13:48.246 align:middle
That's to say that I think my
colleagues around the table,

13:48.246 --> 13:49.936 align:middle
when they submitted their dots,

13:50.256 --> 13:52.366 align:middle
understand the world is
changing quite quickly.

13:52.906 --> 13:55.456 align:middle
And they didn't feel bound
by them six weeks from now

13:55.456 --> 13:57.736 align:middle
or six days from now
and if-in the event

13:57.736 --> 13:59.346 align:middle
that their circumstances change.

13:59.866 --> 14:01.816 align:middle
I'll note a couple other things.

14:01.816 --> 14:04.816 align:middle
What I heard around
the table was,

14:04.936 --> 14:07.216 align:middle
as they submitted
their modal forecasts,

14:07.216 --> 14:09.456 align:middle
their modal forecasts-to
be clear-weren't,

14:09.776 --> 14:12.006 align:middle
this was more likely than not.

14:12.776 --> 14:16.016 align:middle
This was-this was more likely
than their other scenarios.

14:16.516 --> 14:19.546 align:middle
So I didn't hear
tons of conviction.

14:19.546 --> 14:21.606 align:middle
What I heard was
the kind of humility

14:21.606 --> 14:23.626 align:middle
that I think we should have.

14:23.716 --> 14:25.686 align:middle
I did not submit a, a dot.

14:26.036 --> 14:28.986 align:middle
For me, it's not helpful
in the conduct of policy.

14:29.686 --> 14:32.206 align:middle
I suspect, by year-end,
as I mentioned

14:32.206 --> 14:35.066 align:middle
in my opening statements,
there'll be a review

14:35.066 --> 14:40.336 align:middle
about communications broadly,
press conferences, dots,

14:40.926 --> 14:44.726 align:middle
meetings, and the like,
transcripts, minutes.

14:45.016 --> 14:45.976 align:middle
This will be part of that.

14:45.976 --> 14:48.436 align:middle
I don't want to prejudge
the outcomes there,

14:48.566 --> 14:51.416 align:middle
but I'm pretty open minded
about what they could be.

14:52.026 --> 14:54.286 align:middle
And I was just incredibly
impressed

14:54.286 --> 14:55.776 align:middle
over the last couple of days.

14:55.926 --> 14:58.136 align:middle
My colleagues over the
last two days, and frankly,

14:58.136 --> 14:59.866 align:middle
over the first three
weeks I've been here,

15:00.406 --> 15:02.966 align:middle
they've been very
open about changes.

15:02.966 --> 15:03.936 align:middle
Change isn't easy.

15:03.936 --> 15:05.186 align:middle
Change is filled with risk.

15:05.756 --> 15:08.986 align:middle
But our number one goal is
to get monetary policy right.

15:10.006 --> 15:13.466 align:middle
The way to get monetary policy
right is to deliver on the remit

15:13.466 --> 15:16.216 align:middle
that Congress gave us to
deliver on price stability.

15:16.736 --> 15:19.436 align:middle
And there was no disagreement
on any of those points.

15:19.686 --> 15:20.136 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

15:20.136 --> 15:22.676 align:middle
At the risk of possibly
getting the same answer

15:22.676 --> 15:25.396 align:middle
about task forces,
communications,

15:26.046 --> 15:28.506 align:middle
what is your feeling about
these news conferences?

15:28.506 --> 15:33.016 align:middle
Are you going to continue
one after every meeting?

15:33.016 --> 15:34.216 align:middle
Do you think-find them useful?

15:34.596 --> 15:38.186 align:middle
What is the future for the way
Kevin Warsh will communicate?

15:38.626 --> 15:39.006 align:middle
CHARIMAN WARSH.

15:39.006 --> 15:41.576 align:middle
Well, this one's probably got
another 15 or 20 minutes in it,

15:41.576 --> 15:45.686 align:middle
so I don't want to
prejudge the outcome.

15:45.906 --> 15:49.926 align:middle
Press conferences can be a
very useful way to communicate

15:50.046 --> 15:52.606 align:middle
with households,
businesses, and more broadly,

15:52.726 --> 15:54.236 align:middle
through using the likes of you.

15:54.306 --> 15:57.926 align:middle
I had a great old mentor
named George Shultz,

15:57.926 --> 16:01.426 align:middle
and his mantra was, "Press
conferences are useful.

16:01.426 --> 16:02.866 align:middle
But when you have one, you want

16:02.866 --> 16:04.326 align:middle
to make sure you have
something important to say."

16:04.996 --> 16:06.676 align:middle
Today I think we had
something important to say

16:07.406 --> 16:09.906 align:middle
about our commitment to
deliver on price stability,

16:10.606 --> 16:13.386 align:middle
our commitment to rethink
practices with an eye

16:13.386 --> 16:16.586 align:middle
of moving the Fed
forward, and to give you

16:16.586 --> 16:19.026 align:middle
and the American people a sense
that these aren't idle thoughts.

16:19.026 --> 16:22.076 align:middle
These are concrete thoughts
that we're going to seek

16:22.076 --> 16:24.956 align:middle
out the best minds, both
the best thinking inside

16:24.956 --> 16:28.206 align:middle
of the Federal Reserve and the
best people I know in business,

16:28.206 --> 16:30.966 align:middle
and economics, and the
academy, and technology,

16:30.966 --> 16:33.116 align:middle
and the rest, to
share their views.

16:33.536 --> 16:34.136 align:middle
That's what we're going

16:34.136 --> 16:35.666 align:middle
to be doing here,
the pursuit of truth.

16:36.356 --> 16:38.076 align:middle
I think we're going to
come up with some new

16:38.076 --> 16:39.496 align:middle
and interesting things.

16:39.976 --> 16:41.416 align:middle
We made some changes today.

16:41.416 --> 16:43.576 align:middle
I expect more changes
to come, and,

16:43.576 --> 16:47.156 align:middle
and some of those might well be
worthy of a press conference.

16:48.186 --> 16:48.536 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

16:48.536 --> 16:49.036 align:middle
Chris Rugaber.

16:49.856 --> 16:50.486 align:middle
CHRIS RUGABER.

16:51.406 --> 16:54.096 align:middle
Hi. Chris Rugaber
at Associated Press.

16:54.096 --> 16:56.496 align:middle
Thanks for taking our questions.

16:56.716 --> 16:57.626 align:middle
Could you give us a sense

16:57.626 --> 17:00.336 align:middle
of how you see inflation
more in the long term?

17:00.336 --> 17:02.566 align:middle
I know you may not want to
comment on the ups and downs.

17:02.606 --> 17:06.466 align:middle
But is this mainly driven by
energy prices and the Iran war

17:06.466 --> 17:08.226 align:middle
at this point, or do
you have any concerns

17:08.226 --> 17:11.156 align:middle
about underlying inflation
pressures in the economy?

17:11.596 --> 17:11.876 align:middle
Thank you.

17:11.876 --> 17:12.746 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

17:12.746 --> 17:16.146 align:middle
So I can't do much better than,
than the Committee just did,

17:16.146 --> 17:17.686 align:middle
so let me-let me restate it.

17:18.306 --> 17:20.476 align:middle
Inflation remains
elevated relative

17:20.476 --> 17:22.036 align:middle
to the Committee's
2 percent goal,

17:22.476 --> 17:25.736 align:middle
in part reflecting supply shocks
that have driven price increases

17:25.736 --> 17:27.476 align:middle
in certain sectors,
including energy.

17:28.276 --> 17:31.556 align:middle
That paragraph goes on
to say, but to be clear,

17:32.026 --> 17:33.686 align:middle
the Fed'll deliver
price stability.

17:34.306 --> 17:37.476 align:middle
My own judgment is the Committee
spent quite a bit of time,

17:37.646 --> 17:41.196 align:middle
not just in two days but over
iterations of a couple weeks.

17:41.706 --> 17:43.906 align:middle
That's what we're prepared
to say about inflation,

17:43.906 --> 17:47.616 align:middle
but the commitment
to deliver is strong,

17:47.866 --> 17:49.626 align:middle
unanimous, and unambiguous.

17:49.976 --> 17:52.426 align:middle
And that's, I think, an
important message we've missed

17:52.426 --> 17:55.196 align:middle
for five years, and, and
we're going to fix that.

17:55.196 --> 17:55.626 align:middle
CHRIS RUGABER.

17:55.786 --> 17:56.386 align:middle
Well, great.

17:56.386 --> 17:59.336 align:middle
And then just on your-the,
the data task force

17:59.336 --> 18:01.106 align:middle
and everything else, I
mean, generally speaking,

18:01.676 --> 18:05.236 align:middle
I think people feel the-feel the
Fed looks at everything already,

18:05.236 --> 18:07.556 align:middle
certainly that was
the sense from before.

18:09.096 --> 18:11.986 align:middle
What's-is there data that you
feel is not given enough weight?

18:11.986 --> 18:14.106 align:middle
I mean, you mentioned the
trimmed mean in the past,

18:14.106 --> 18:16.416 align:middle
but again, that's well known
to certainly most Fed members.

18:16.416 --> 18:19.996 align:middle
So what is that task force
looking at and, and what,

18:20.146 --> 18:21.586 align:middle
what might be the-I mean,
I know you don't want

18:21.586 --> 18:24.896 align:middle
to prejudge the outcome, but
are there examples of data

18:24.896 --> 18:27.296 align:middle
that you expect might
be given more weight?

18:27.576 --> 18:27.826 align:middle
Thank you.

18:27.826 --> 18:28.256 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

18:28.256 --> 18:30.696 align:middle
So you're answering my question.

18:30.696 --> 18:32.806 align:middle
So let me say, I don't want
to prejudge the outcome.

18:33.286 --> 18:35.466 align:middle
I also don't want to say too
much about what they're going

18:35.466 --> 18:37.086 align:middle
to do because I still
have a phone call or two

18:37.086 --> 18:39.816 align:middle
to make before I've nailed down
the people that are doing that.

18:40.226 --> 18:43.116 align:middle
I'm interested [in] what
the outside experts' view is

18:43.116 --> 18:43.886 align:middle
on the subject.

18:44.686 --> 18:51.086 align:middle
I'll say this generally: Most
of the data that central bankers

18:51.086 --> 18:52.766 align:middle
and other government officials

18:52.816 --> 18:55.366 align:middle
in the United States
consume come

18:55.366 --> 18:59.146 align:middle
with old-fashioned survey
methods, national accounts

18:59.536 --> 19:03.986 align:middle
of what the U.S. economy looks
like that looks very little

19:03.986 --> 19:08.936 align:middle
like the U.S. economy
in 2026, survey methods

19:08.936 --> 19:12.576 align:middle
that don't have response rates
that we need, asking questions

19:12.576 --> 19:14.736 align:middle
that might've been quite
applicable a generation ago

19:14.736 --> 19:15.976 align:middle
that are less applicable now.

19:16.746 --> 19:19.566 align:middle
So even inside of
official statistics,

19:20.046 --> 19:22.716 align:middle
I would be open minded
if the task force

19:22.806 --> 19:26.036 align:middle
and our own best thinking
had recommendations how those

19:26.036 --> 19:29.476 align:middle
official statistics can be
brought up to a standard of,

19:29.726 --> 19:31.936 align:middle
of our time using
new analytic methods.

19:32.546 --> 19:37.016 align:middle
I'd also say this: Almost every
private company CEO that's

19:37.016 --> 19:39.686 align:middle
running his or her
business are doing

19:39.686 --> 19:43.286 align:middle
so with real-time
information that isn't subject

19:43.286 --> 19:46.576 align:middle
to much revision, that is
telling them what just happened

19:46.576 --> 19:47.526 align:middle
at that very moment.

19:48.406 --> 19:51.286 align:middle
As you know, there are
normal long and variable lags

19:51.286 --> 19:53.046 align:middle
in the conduct of
monetary policy.

19:53.796 --> 19:57.566 align:middle
What we're really interested in
is what's happening right now.

19:57.596 --> 20:00.576 align:middle
What we're less interested
in is echoes of history.

20:01.106 --> 20:03.966 align:middle
And you're hearing from my
answer that some of the data

20:03.966 --> 20:07.986 align:middle
that we receive, that we're
waiting on, the first Friday

20:07.986 --> 20:10.576 align:middle
after the month, the payroll
index, or something else,

20:11.106 --> 20:13.786 align:middle
that might be an echo of
history that's quite useful

20:13.876 --> 20:15.176 align:middle
on its third revision.

20:15.556 --> 20:18.026 align:middle
We need to take those error
bounds down because we have

20:18.026 --> 20:20.726 align:middle
to make hard decisions
in real time.

20:21.436 --> 20:24.516 align:middle
I'm really open minded
that there is a lot

20:24.516 --> 20:28.376 align:middle
of new data sources that we can
learn from the private sector,

20:28.716 --> 20:30.626 align:middle
from reforms in the
official sector,

20:30.886 --> 20:34.836 align:middle
and new analytic techniques
that are far more refined

20:34.836 --> 20:36.086 align:middle
than asking a simple question

20:36.086 --> 20:38.346 align:middle
about whether something
was core or noncore.

20:40.036 --> 20:40.456 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

20:40.846 --> 20:41.496 align:middle
Edward. EDWARD LAWRENCE.

20:42.126 --> 20:43.166 align:middle
Thanks. Welcome, Mr. Chairman.

20:43.166 --> 20:44.766 align:middle
Edward Lawrence with
Fox Business.

20:45.126 --> 20:49.716 align:middle
So, if you don't give a lot
of ongoing forward guidance,

20:49.716 --> 20:51.526 align:middle
won't the markets
have more volatility?

20:51.526 --> 20:53.526 align:middle
And shouldn't Americans
have more access

20:53.526 --> 20:55.376 align:middle
into what you're
thinking going forward?

20:55.996 --> 20:56.586 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

20:56.936 --> 21:01.146 align:middle
So I think financial
markets perform best

21:01.476 --> 21:04.126 align:middle
when they react to
incoming data.

21:05.336 --> 21:08.656 align:middle
I think they-the financial
markets work less efficiently

21:09.186 --> 21:12.616 align:middle
when they ask a question: How
will the Federal Reserve react

21:12.616 --> 21:13.936 align:middle
to that incoming information?

21:14.816 --> 21:19.246 align:middle
The more that markets are paying
attention to what's happening

21:19.246 --> 21:22.576 align:middle
in the real economy,
deciding what's good data

21:22.626 --> 21:23.966 align:middle
and what's less good data,

21:24.436 --> 21:27.846 align:middle
the more financial markets can
price what they believe is the

21:27.846 --> 21:30.256 align:middle
most likely and what
are the tail risks.

21:30.936 --> 21:34.426 align:middle
Financial market prices are
probably the most important

21:34.426 --> 21:38.066 align:middle
source of information to
guide central bankers.

21:38.806 --> 21:41.726 align:middle
But when all the financial
markets are doing is reflecting

21:41.726 --> 21:45.296 align:middle
back what we've said, then we're
taking the most important source

21:45.296 --> 21:47.426 align:middle
of information and
we're being blind to it.

21:47.966 --> 21:51.406 align:middle
I'd like us to create a system
where those blinders come off,

21:51.766 --> 21:53.496 align:middle
where markets are following data

21:53.496 --> 21:55.716 align:middle
that they efficiently
think is reliable.

21:56.246 --> 21:57.616 align:middle
And they'll be watching data.

21:57.676 --> 21:58.946 align:middle
We'll be watching data.

21:59.256 --> 22:01.136 align:middle
They'll come with
better information

22:01.136 --> 22:02.596 align:middle
through market prices to us.

22:03.016 --> 22:04.726 align:middle
We can make more
informed decisions.

22:05.286 --> 22:07.416 align:middle
But ultimately, the goal
that I said at the outset,

22:07.606 --> 22:10.916 align:middle
deliver on the price-stability
objective that Congress told us

22:10.916 --> 22:13.226 align:middle
to do and that we've got to
get in the business of doing.

22:13.226 --> 22:13.576 align:middle
EDWARD LAWRENCE.

22:13.666 --> 22:15.816 align:middle
If I could take you in
the meeting a little bit.

22:15.816 --> 22:17.516 align:middle
So your first meeting, the,

22:17.516 --> 22:20.146 align:middle
the Board members
seemed fairly hawkish

22:20.146 --> 22:22.196 align:middle
when you listen to-in
general, when you listen

22:22.196 --> 22:22.686 align:middle
to what they're saying.

22:22.686 --> 22:25.426 align:middle
Was there any discussion of a
rate cut going forward today?

22:25.976 --> 22:26.646 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

22:27.666 --> 22:29.696 align:middle
There was one proposal
on the table.

22:30.446 --> 22:33.196 align:middle
There was no discussion
of any other proposals.

22:33.696 --> 22:37.356 align:middle
The discussion on that proposal,
I would say, was quite limited.

22:37.816 --> 22:41.936 align:middle
The group was unanimous
and unambiguous on it.

22:41.936 --> 22:45.536 align:middle
It has been the practice of
this central bank and others

22:45.736 --> 22:47.426 align:middle
to have a range of alternatives.

22:49.056 --> 22:50.506 align:middle
Today we had one.

22:50.506 --> 22:53.426 align:middle
I thought it furthered
discussion, deepened it

22:54.006 --> 22:57.436 align:middle
and made it clear
what we needed to do

22:57.436 --> 22:59.096 align:middle
and how we need to deliver.

22:59.096 --> 23:01.446 align:middle
I wouldn't prejudge what
happens in the future,

23:01.446 --> 23:04.026 align:middle
but there was only one
big subject for us.

23:04.266 --> 23:05.076 align:middle
We took it on.

23:05.496 --> 23:08.116 align:middle
We had a good family fight
on it for a couple of days,

23:08.116 --> 23:09.946 align:middle
and we ended up, I
think, in a better place.

23:11.626 --> 23:13.846 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

23:14.496 --> 23:15.436 align:middle
Claire. CLAIRE JONES.

23:15.566 --> 23:16.126 align:middle
Thanks a lot.

23:16.126 --> 23:17.736 align:middle
Claire Jones, Financial Times.

23:18.466 --> 23:20.766 align:middle
You know, coming to this
"blind," reading this very nice,

23:20.766 --> 23:23.336 align:middle
short statement that I
think we've all appreciated

23:23.336 --> 23:30.046 align:middle
in the room, one might wonder
why you didn't raise rates today

23:30.046 --> 23:33.706 align:middle
considering what you're
saying here about the,

23:33.706 --> 23:37.406 align:middle
the risks to U.S.
inflation and your mandate.

23:37.996 --> 23:41.446 align:middle
I guess why not and what
would you need to see

23:41.446 --> 23:43.406 align:middle
in order to get to that place?

23:44.226 --> 23:48.076 align:middle
And secondly, on
your task forces,

23:48.076 --> 23:50.436 align:middle
are there any best
practices of the central banks

23:50.516 --> 23:51.786 align:middle
that you'd consider looking at?

23:52.006 --> 23:52.436 align:middle
Thank you.

23:52.436 --> 23:52.826 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

23:53.016 --> 23:54.616 align:middle
Yeah. I'm glad they're
in the practice

23:54.616 --> 23:56.326 align:middle
of giving you two
questions, because my answer

23:56.326 --> 23:58.136 align:middle
to your first question
was to be very curt.

23:58.296 --> 24:00.526 align:middle
I've got nothing more to say
than the statement itself.

24:01.346 --> 24:04.926 align:middle
And to the point of the question
I got before: Market reactions

24:04.926 --> 24:08.636 align:middle
to what we say, unfiltered,
I think is more helpful than,

24:08.936 --> 24:10.186 align:middle
having delivered a statement,

24:10.186 --> 24:12.056 align:middle
me then improvising
further upon it.

24:12.796 --> 24:14.756 align:middle
Best practices of task forces,

24:14.826 --> 24:17.006 align:middle
this is a subject I've
thought some about.

24:17.006 --> 24:19.716 align:middle
I've been on a task
force or two in my life.

24:20.276 --> 24:24.256 align:middle
Best practice, find
the best minds.

24:24.406 --> 24:28.726 align:middle
Ensure that the task forces
have a range of people,

24:28.726 --> 24:32.746 align:middle
both by backgrounds and
predispositions, so they, too,

24:32.746 --> 24:35.686 align:middle
can have a bit of
a family fight.

24:36.116 --> 24:38.596 align:middle
Make sure when you
establish a task force

24:39.126 --> 24:40.666 align:middle
that the group that's
going to be the recipient

24:40.666 --> 24:42.266 align:middle
of the information feels

24:42.266 --> 24:43.746 align:middle
as if they've got some
equities in it, too.

24:44.566 --> 24:47.386 align:middle
That's why we're looking
for-haven't done the final roll

24:47.386 --> 24:50.186 align:middle
call-some of the most
significant talent we have

24:50.186 --> 24:52.906 align:middle
in the building and across
the Reserve Banks on each

24:52.906 --> 24:55.916 align:middle
of these and, in some sense,
seconding them to this group

24:55.916 --> 25:01.076 align:middle
for a period of some number
of months so that the leaders

25:01.076 --> 25:06.366 align:middle
of the task force know what the
most analytical central bank

25:06.366 --> 25:07.936 align:middle
in the world thinks about that.

25:08.436 --> 25:09.436 align:middle
They can reflect on it.

25:09.436 --> 25:10.866 align:middle
And a final best practice,

25:11.526 --> 25:14.546 align:middle
we're not outsourcing
decisions to anybody.

25:14.946 --> 25:17.396 align:middle
Administrations past
and present,

25:17.396 --> 25:19.056 align:middle
Reserve Banks have
chosen a group

25:19.056 --> 25:20.836 align:middle
of 19 people around the table.

25:21.036 --> 25:22.336 align:middle
These will be our decisions.

25:22.746 --> 25:25.216 align:middle
We can agree to some
of the recommendations,

25:25.216 --> 25:28.296 align:middle
disagree with others, have a
good family fight about it.

25:28.296 --> 25:31.166 align:middle
But what comes from them
will, I hope and believe,

25:31.346 --> 25:34.176 align:middle
make the discussion we have
internally better, stronger,

25:34.646 --> 25:39.286 align:middle
more of a dialectic, so
that we can finally deliver

25:39.286 --> 25:40.586 align:middle
on that price-stability
objective.

25:40.586 --> 25:41.366 align:middle
CLAIRE JONES.

25:41.366 --> 25:44.016 align:middle
Just a quick follow-up on
your, your markets' point,

25:44.016 --> 25:46.386 align:middle
if you look at two-year yields,
they're really suggesting

25:46.386 --> 25:49.676 align:middle
that markets think more
tightening is needed.

25:49.956 --> 25:52.726 align:middle
Would that be your read on what
the two-year yield is saying

25:52.726 --> 25:53.106 align:middle
as well?

25:53.106 --> 25:53.446 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

25:53.446 --> 25:54.626 align:middle
We were in such a good place.

25:54.626 --> 25:56.576 align:middle
This is why we don't do
third questions, I presume.

25:56.906 --> 26:00.186 align:middle
I'm not going to offer any
commentary on market reaction

26:00.186 --> 26:03.076 align:middle
over the last 30 or 60 minutes.

26:03.216 --> 26:06.726 align:middle
What we've given
markets is a new chapter

26:06.726 --> 26:09.406 align:middle
for the central bank,
some fresh thinking.

26:10.316 --> 26:13.196 align:middle
What we've given markets, and
households, and businesses,

26:13.196 --> 26:18.096 align:middle
I think, is a commitment to
ask ourselves hard questions,

26:18.566 --> 26:22.196 align:middle
such that we can deliver on the
promises that we've made before.

26:22.796 --> 26:27.776 align:middle
This is a lot of change for
financial markets to digest.

26:28.206 --> 26:31.596 align:middle
I wouldn't be particularly
intrigued by how they react

26:31.596 --> 26:34.616 align:middle
in the first several minutes
or even first several days.

26:35.036 --> 26:38.356 align:middle
What I think is most important
is that financial markets,

26:38.486 --> 26:41.396 align:middle
and at least as important,
households and businesses,

26:41.396 --> 26:44.996 align:middle
know that the central bank will
deliver on price stability.

26:44.996 --> 26:46.126 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

26:46.196 --> 26:47.806 align:middle
Brian. BRIAN CHEUNG.

26:48.616 --> 26:49.266 align:middle
Hi, Chairman Warsh.

26:49.366 --> 26:50.376 align:middle
Brian Cheung with NBC News.

26:50.376 --> 26:51.626 align:middle
Thank you for taking
our questions.

26:51.626 --> 26:53.816 align:middle
So, when you say that we've
dropped forward guidance,

26:53.816 --> 26:56.446 align:middle
for the layperson, that might
sound like the Fed's going

26:56.446 --> 26:58.266 align:middle
to say less or offer
less insight

26:58.266 --> 27:00.226 align:middle
into where their
borrowing costs might go.

27:00.646 --> 27:02.396 align:middle
So, for the person that
maybe you might run

27:02.396 --> 27:05.306 align:middle
into at the grocery store,
where the price tags are rising

27:05.306 --> 27:07.396 align:middle
at a faster pace than
their wages at the moment,

27:07.736 --> 27:08.756 align:middle
how would you explain
it to them?

27:08.756 --> 27:10.816 align:middle
I don't know if "task force"
might be the answer there,

27:10.866 --> 27:13.366 align:middle
but how would you kind
of communicate this era,

27:13.366 --> 27:14.526 align:middle
this chapter of the Fed?

27:14.786 --> 27:15.186 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

27:15.186 --> 27:18.686 align:middle
If I told somebody in the milk
aisle that I had a task force

27:18.686 --> 27:21.016 align:middle
for that, I think that would
be doing a very poor job,

27:21.016 --> 27:22.606 align:middle
so I appreciate it.

27:23.516 --> 27:26.336 align:middle
If I saw somebody in the
grocery store, what I would say

27:26.336 --> 27:31.156 align:middle
to them is that we cannot
have a very significant effect

27:31.236 --> 27:32.676 align:middle
on particular prices.

27:33.306 --> 27:37.016 align:middle
The price of oil in the
markets today or even the price

27:37.016 --> 27:41.026 align:middle
of a dozen eggs, that does not
have first-order consequences

27:41.026 --> 27:41.706 align:middle
to what we're doing.

27:42.366 --> 27:44.276 align:middle
But we do have a really
important job there,

27:44.656 --> 27:48.146 align:middle
and it's to make sure that
those changes in oil, or beef,

27:48.146 --> 27:51.266 align:middle
or eggs, or milk don't
broaden in the economy,

27:51.456 --> 27:53.406 align:middle
don't have second- and
third-order effects.

27:53.836 --> 27:54.626 align:middle
That's our job.

27:55.116 --> 27:56.076 align:middle
That's our commitment.

27:56.136 --> 27:58.066 align:middle
That's our capability, and
we're going to deliver on it.

27:58.656 --> 27:59.206 align:middle
BRIAN CHEUNG.

27:59.206 --> 28:00.396 align:middle
And then is the Fed's
relationship

28:00.476 --> 28:03.156 align:middle
with the Treasury
also under review?

28:03.156 --> 28:05.466 align:middle
There was the normal
breakfast meetings

28:05.466 --> 28:06.786 align:middle
with the Treasury Secretary.

28:07.156 --> 28:08.896 align:middle
Is that something you
intend to continue doing,

28:08.896 --> 28:10.536 align:middle
and have you had
conversations with the President

28:10.536 --> 28:11.176 align:middle
since your swearing in?

28:11.376 --> 28:11.776 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

28:11.776 --> 28:14.706 align:middle
So, on the President, I, I
don't have anything for you.

28:14.706 --> 28:17.406 align:middle
With respect to the
Treasury Secretary,

28:17.406 --> 28:19.676 align:middle
he has been posting
pictures of our breakfasts,

28:19.676 --> 28:22.746 align:middle
so I don't think I can-I
don't think I can deny that.

28:22.966 --> 28:24.976 align:middle
The long tradition at
the central bank is

28:24.976 --> 28:28.556 align:middle
that the Fed Chairman and the
Treasury Secretary meet weekly.

28:28.556 --> 28:30.956 align:middle
I think we've pulled off
three of those so far.

28:30.956 --> 28:32.936 align:middle
I believe he's overseas
this week,

28:32.936 --> 28:34.786 align:middle
so this'll be the
exception to the rule.

28:35.256 --> 28:37.566 align:middle
I think they're very
useful discussions.

28:37.996 --> 28:43.136 align:middle
The central bank's
objectives and our roles

28:43.136 --> 28:45.446 align:middle
and responsibilities
are quite delineated

28:45.516 --> 28:46.696 align:middle
from the fiscal authorities.

28:47.536 --> 28:50.966 align:middle
And, in my view, monetary policy
is independent in the conduct

28:50.966 --> 28:54.036 align:middle
of what we do, but that doesn't
mean we're not interested

28:54.206 --> 28:56.106 align:middle
in what's happening with
the fiscal authorities.

28:56.426 --> 28:59.066 align:middle
The way I think about it
is this central bank needs

28:59.066 --> 29:02.706 align:middle
to have a wide lens
but a narrower remit.

29:03.256 --> 29:04.816 align:middle
We need to be quite interested

29:04.816 --> 29:07.376 align:middle
in what's happening
in the world.

29:07.376 --> 29:10.186 align:middle
I won't be breaking any
news here to suggest

29:10.186 --> 29:12.236 align:middle
that I'm quite interested what's
happening in the Middle East.

29:12.646 --> 29:15.856 align:middle
That does have some
effect on our day job.

29:15.966 --> 29:18.526 align:middle
It doesn't mean it's
our responsibility,

29:18.526 --> 29:20.336 align:middle
but I think we're going
to keep a wide lens.

29:20.336 --> 29:22.496 align:middle
And my meetings with
Secretary Bessent

29:22.496 --> 29:24.896 align:middle
to this point have helped
widen that aperture.

29:25.396 --> 29:26.686 align:middle
So we're aware of things

29:26.686 --> 29:29.116 align:middle
that could affect our day
job, even if it isn't.

29:29.206 --> 29:30.406 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

29:30.676 --> 29:33.206 align:middle
Steve. STEVE LIESMAN.

29:33.546 --> 29:34.376 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

29:34.376 --> 29:35.406 align:middle
Mr. Chair-Mr.

29:35.496 --> 29:37.986 align:middle
Chairman, thank you
for taking my question.

29:38.426 --> 29:42.826 align:middle
You had said in the-before
you became Chairman

29:43.266 --> 29:46.676 align:middle
that you thought productivity
was a reason why the Federal

29:46.676 --> 29:48.626 align:middle
Reserve could lower
interest rates.

29:49.156 --> 29:50.676 align:middle
Do you still believe
that to be the case?

29:51.486 --> 29:51.976 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

29:51.976 --> 29:54.176 align:middle
So the Committee
had a discussion

29:54.176 --> 29:56.616 align:middle
of productivity today.

29:56.616 --> 29:57.886 align:middle
AI came up.

29:58.926 --> 30:02.416 align:middle
The way I thought about
it before and socialized

30:02.416 --> 30:06.126 align:middle
with the group is that
artificial intelligence,

30:06.126 --> 30:08.906 align:middle
the latest generation of
general purpose technology,

30:09.586 --> 30:14.726 align:middle
is perhaps as important a change
in the economy and business

30:14.726 --> 30:16.896 align:middle
and households that we've
had in my adult lifetime.

30:17.596 --> 30:21.996 align:middle
It is filled with both a huge
opportunity and with risks.

30:22.456 --> 30:24.666 align:middle
I take both of those
very seriously.

30:25.966 --> 30:30.846 align:middle
You may have heard me say before
that AI is shorthand perhaps

30:30.846 --> 30:31.996 align:middle
for American ingenuity.

30:32.886 --> 30:34.966 align:middle
That doesn't mean that
it's going to be easy.

30:34.966 --> 30:37.326 align:middle
That certainly doesn't mean
it's not going to be disruptive.

30:38.086 --> 30:39.526 align:middle
But over the long term,

30:39.816 --> 30:42.946 align:middle
my conviction-and I heard
quite a bit of support for this

30:42.946 --> 30:46.236 align:middle
around the Committee today-is
the United States is a winner

30:46.816 --> 30:47.966 align:middle
as we go down this.

30:48.046 --> 30:51.216 align:middle
The United States is ultimately
going to be better off in that.

30:52.176 --> 30:55.586 align:middle
Now, to bring that back to
the conduct of policy, timing,

30:55.656 --> 31:00.296 align:middle
scale, speed, implications
for output and employment,

31:00.296 --> 31:03.556 align:middle
it's one of the things we
have a task force to do.

31:03.736 --> 31:04.526 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

31:04.976 --> 31:07.036 align:middle
[Laughter] If you don't mind a
follow-up from the other side,

31:07.036 --> 31:10.026 align:middle
which is that, when you look
at the strong job growth that's

31:10.026 --> 31:11.676 align:middle
out there, the elevated
inflation,

31:11.676 --> 31:14.946 align:middle
GDP seems to be going pretty
good and the stock market seems

31:14.946 --> 31:17.776 align:middle
to be soaring, do you
look around this economy

31:17.776 --> 31:19.886 align:middle
and see the funds rate
being restrictive?

31:20.926 --> 31:21.416 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

31:21.626 --> 31:23.556 align:middle
So that's your second question.

31:23.556 --> 31:26.116 align:middle
I'm going to give the same
answer that I gave before.

31:26.306 --> 31:28.796 align:middle
I'd say, as I think about
the conduct of policy,

31:29.336 --> 31:31.176 align:middle
what matters is what's
the effect of policy,

31:31.226 --> 31:33.356 align:middle
not what do we say,
but what happens.

31:33.796 --> 31:35.946 align:middle
And the best way I can
describe is, it's uneven.

31:35.946 --> 31:38.586 align:middle
I do see some restrictiveness
on things like housing.

31:38.586 --> 31:41.926 align:middle
It's hard to use those
same words anywhere else.

31:42.296 --> 31:43.876 align:middle
I'll just make one other point.

31:45.016 --> 31:48.106 align:middle
You talked about one
of our dual mandates

31:48.206 --> 31:49.556 align:middle
and the employment side.

31:50.406 --> 31:53.246 align:middle
I don't believe that
we have a cruel choice.

31:53.706 --> 31:57.956 align:middle
I don't share the view that was
expressed a few generations ago

31:58.686 --> 32:02.006 align:middle
that Federal Reserve chairmen
show up a podium like this

32:02.076 --> 32:03.796 align:middle
and say, "You've got to choose.

32:04.576 --> 32:07.796 align:middle
And you're going to have to
decide whether you're willing

32:07.796 --> 32:11.706 align:middle
to tolerate higher inflation
to put more people at work."

32:12.336 --> 32:13.396 align:middle
I don't believe in that.

32:13.786 --> 32:18.836 align:middle
What I believe is, if we do our
job, we can make strong growth,

32:20.326 --> 32:24.606 align:middle
low prices, and strong
employment mutually compatible.

32:25.286 --> 32:27.716 align:middle
And so what you heard from
the Committee today is,

32:27.716 --> 32:30.126 align:middle
we've got some work to do on
this price-stability front.

32:31.026 --> 32:31.606 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

32:31.816 --> 32:33.326 align:middle
Nick. NICK TIMIRAOS.

32:34.136 --> 32:34.426 align:middle
Thank you.

32:34.426 --> 32:35.866 align:middle
Nick Timiraos with
Wall Street Journal.

32:36.476 --> 32:40.126 align:middle
Chairman Warsh, you've said
repeatedly credibility is earned

32:40.126 --> 32:40.906 align:middle
by delivering.

32:41.266 --> 32:44.356 align:middle
If credibility requires
delivering, the move would be

32:44.356 --> 32:46.326 align:middle
to tighten or at
least to threaten to.

32:46.666 --> 32:47.816 align:middle
Now, you didn't do that today.

32:47.816 --> 32:50.276 align:middle
Why not? CHAIRMAN WARSH.

32:50.966 --> 32:53.836 align:middle
That judgment you expressed
was not expressed by any

32:53.836 --> 32:55.596 align:middle
of the 19 people
around the table.

32:56.196 --> 32:58.116 align:middle
We'll be meeting in six weeks,

32:58.116 --> 32:59.246 align:middle
and we'll take up
the issue again.

32:59.586 --> 33:00.386 align:middle
NICK TIMIRAOS.

33:00.386 --> 33:01.876 align:middle
And, and, if I could
ask about AI,

33:01.876 --> 33:04.946 align:middle
the buildout is generating
enormous demand right now,

33:04.946 --> 33:06.566 align:middle
cap-ex, data centers, power.

33:07.166 --> 33:09.296 align:middle
The productivity payoff
may be further out.

33:09.296 --> 33:11.996 align:middle
So, in your judgment
today, is AI adding more

33:11.996 --> 33:13.346 align:middle
to demand or to supply?

33:14.416 --> 33:15.196 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

33:15.196 --> 33:16.216 align:middle
It's a good question.

33:16.976 --> 33:20.586 align:middle
At the central bank and in
the economics profession,

33:21.056 --> 33:24.866 align:middle
what we spend most of our
time doing is counting demand.

33:24.866 --> 33:25.816 align:middle
It's easier.

33:26.516 --> 33:27.176 align:middle
We can see it.

33:27.176 --> 33:27.886 align:middle
We can count it.

33:27.886 --> 33:28.656 align:middle
We can check it.

33:28.836 --> 33:29.666 align:middle
We can revise it.

33:30.756 --> 33:33.266 align:middle
What we do, though,
is we infer supply.

33:34.476 --> 33:38.356 align:middle
You'll notice in the
second paragraph of what one

33:38.356 --> 33:40.826 align:middle
of your colleagues described
as "a very short statement,"

33:41.826 --> 33:45.836 align:middle
we have a sentence on the
demand side and a sentence

33:45.836 --> 33:47.686 align:middle
about the same length
on the supply side.

33:48.086 --> 33:48.946 align:middle
They're both important.

33:49.496 --> 33:51.276 align:middle
Just because we can
count one better

33:51.276 --> 33:52.626 align:middle
than the other doesn't
mean we're going

33:52.626 --> 33:54.056 align:middle
to favor one more
than the other.

33:54.906 --> 33:58.056 align:middle
With respect to AI and
the growth of data centers

33:58.056 --> 34:01.486 align:middle
and infrastructure around it,
we're counting the demand side,

34:01.846 --> 34:04.936 align:middle
and it is no doubt
showing up in GDP figures.

34:05.756 --> 34:10.106 align:middle
We can be less certain when
we infer the timing and extent

34:10.106 --> 34:11.556 align:middle
of the growth on
the supply side.

34:12.476 --> 34:15.776 align:middle
It may well be an intuition the
supply side is going to expand,

34:15.776 --> 34:16.826 align:middle
but it'll take longer.

34:17.306 --> 34:19.386 align:middle
But just describe it
this way: There's a race

34:19.386 --> 34:20.606 align:middle
between supply and demand.

34:21.346 --> 34:23.596 align:middle
Milton Friedman says that
the only thing we know

34:23.596 --> 34:26.356 align:middle
about economics is that there's
a supply line and a demand line

34:26.356 --> 34:27.526 align:middle
and they ultimately cross.

34:27.956 --> 34:30.216 align:middle
When they cross, what are
the implications for policy?

34:30.936 --> 34:33.416 align:middle
The good news for you is, we
have a task force for that.

34:33.636 --> 34:34.526 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

34:35.256 --> 34:38.606 align:middle
Andrew. ANDREW ACKERMAN.

34:38.606 --> 34:39.396 align:middle
Thanks, Mr. Chairman.

34:39.396 --> 34:43.296 align:middle
It sounded like on the
task force on data that,

34:43.436 --> 34:45.456 align:middle
that you were looking
at overhauling

34:45.456 --> 34:48.066 align:middle
or completely overhauling the
system of national accounts,

34:48.066 --> 34:50.176 align:middle
the way that government
monitors the economy.

34:50.176 --> 34:51.156 align:middle
Is that your ambition?

34:52.026 --> 34:53.216 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

34:53.586 --> 34:54.326 align:middle
In a word, no.

34:54.326 --> 34:59.466 align:middle
In a few words, much of
this data gathering happens

34:59.546 --> 35:03.756 align:middle
in other government agencies to
which we owe a tremendous amount

35:03.756 --> 35:06.406 align:middle
of respect, a tremendous
amount of deference.

35:06.916 --> 35:10.156 align:middle
But, if in the course of this,
we come up with recommendations,

35:10.716 --> 35:14.906 align:middle
which Fed staff have already
begun to develop, about things

35:14.906 --> 35:18.846 align:middle
that they could be doing to
help inform us as policymakers,

35:18.846 --> 35:19.906 align:middle
we're not going to hesitate.

35:20.556 --> 35:24.456 align:middle
Again, I don't want to try
to delineate the four corners

35:24.456 --> 35:26.656 align:middle
of the research of the
task force on data,

35:27.296 --> 35:30.896 align:middle
but I do think there will be a
review of official statistics,

35:31.616 --> 35:32.976 align:middle
and at least as important,

35:32.976 --> 35:35.076 align:middle
a view of bringing
the best practices

35:35.076 --> 35:39.336 align:middle
from the private sector and new
analytical tools made possible

35:39.336 --> 35:42.556 align:middle
by AI, so we can forge
these into a fabric

35:42.636 --> 35:45.116 align:middle
that gives us better
real-time information.

35:45.116 --> 35:48.386 align:middle
So, as I mentioned before,
when we're making decisions,

35:48.706 --> 35:52.306 align:middle
we're making decisions that we'd
say are real contemporaneous

35:52.306 --> 35:55.586 align:middle
data, not data that we
call "contemporaneous."

35:55.926 --> 35:57.206 align:middle
It's really an echo of history.

35:57.646 --> 35:58.326 align:middle
ANDREW ACKERMAN.

35:58.596 --> 35:59.186 align:middle
Okay. Thanks.

35:59.226 --> 36:01.816 align:middle
The other question I
wanted to ask is related

36:01.816 --> 36:03.386 align:middle
to the building renovations.

36:03.726 --> 36:06.346 align:middle
Are you considering any
changes to the renovations,

36:06.346 --> 36:09.176 align:middle
the projects, just in light of
the fact that they became kind

36:09.176 --> 36:11.096 align:middle
of a political football
in the last year?

36:11.576 --> 36:12.656 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

36:12.656 --> 36:15.176 align:middle
I heard something about that.

36:15.176 --> 36:18.076 align:middle
I don't think I'm breaking
any news, but my view is,

36:18.076 --> 36:21.216 align:middle
when you show up at a new
institution, you should go meet

36:21.216 --> 36:25.096 align:middle
with the Inspector General, just
as a matter of good practice.

36:26.106 --> 36:28.346 align:middle
It's a practice that
I hope to continue.

36:28.346 --> 36:30.616 align:middle
I've had one meeting with
the Inspector General,

36:31.166 --> 36:33.596 align:middle
and he told me what I
believe the world knows,

36:33.596 --> 36:37.016 align:middle
which he'll be coming out
with a report on the building

36:37.016 --> 36:41.566 align:middle
and the building projects at
some point later this summer.

36:41.656 --> 36:44.146 align:middle
And I'll be interested
in reading the report.

36:44.646 --> 36:48.026 align:middle
From my perspective, with
a forward-looking glance,

36:48.516 --> 36:51.196 align:middle
is there anything that we can
be doing or should be doing

36:51.756 --> 36:54.266 align:middle
from this moment until the
completion of the project

36:54.846 --> 36:57.586 align:middle
to do what we can to be good
stewards of taxpayer money

36:57.586 --> 37:00.066 align:middle
and make sure that
we're delivering

37:00.066 --> 37:02.026 align:middle
on the promises that we made?

37:02.366 --> 37:03.616 align:middle
So more work to do.

37:04.016 --> 37:06.006 align:middle
You might not be surprised,
in the first few weeks,

37:06.006 --> 37:08.196 align:middle
I've been somewhat
preoccupied on other matters,

37:08.196 --> 37:10.876 align:middle
but I promise to, to
get to the full breadth

37:10.956 --> 37:13.486 align:middle
of the Fed's tasks
in the weeks ahead.

37:14.646 --> 37:17.786 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

37:17.876 --> 37:18.866 align:middle
Victoria. VICTORIA GUIDA.

37:18.866 --> 37:21.126 align:middle
Hi, Victoria Guida
with Politico.

37:21.126 --> 37:23.636 align:middle
So I know that you did
not submit a forecast,

37:23.636 --> 37:26.046 align:middle
but you are the person
who is authorized to speak

37:26.046 --> 37:27.196 align:middle
on behalf of the FOMC.

37:27.196 --> 37:29.876 align:middle
So I'm wondering if you
could tell us, in the SEP,

37:30.056 --> 37:32.816 align:middle
the increase in the
expectations for inflation,

37:33.306 --> 37:36.816 align:middle
is that all because
of the Iran war?

37:36.816 --> 37:39.996 align:middle
What was the discussion
around what-the expectations

37:39.996 --> 37:41.286 align:middle
for inflation being higher

37:41.286 --> 37:43.066 align:middle
and also potentially
growth being slower?

37:43.896 --> 37:44.566 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

37:44.806 --> 37:51.226 align:middle
So my read of what I heard
in the room reflected,

37:51.336 --> 37:53.266 align:middle
I must admit, in the SEPs,

37:53.756 --> 37:57.026 align:middle
is half of my colleagues
thought the policy rate,

37:57.026 --> 38:00.166 align:middle
given all those developments,
should be at this level or lower

38:00.646 --> 38:03.556 align:middle
between now and year-end and
the other half thought higher.

38:04.106 --> 38:07.546 align:middle
That 19th voter was me,
and I didn't submit one.

38:07.886 --> 38:12.716 align:middle
There's a range of views
on the questions of first-

38:12.716 --> 38:18.276 align:middle
and second-round effects,
no resolution or conviction.

38:18.966 --> 38:21.266 align:middle
But we'll be meeting
again in six weeks.

38:21.846 --> 38:23.646 align:middle
I think we're going
to know more then.

38:24.246 --> 38:27.846 align:middle
And I think that my
colleagues are very attentive

38:27.846 --> 38:30.136 align:middle
to incoming developments
between now and then.

38:30.636 --> 38:31.056 align:middle
VICTORIA GUIDA.

38:31.056 --> 38:32.846 align:middle
And can I just-quick
follow-up on the SEP?

38:32.846 --> 38:33.166 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

38:33.166 --> 38:33.746 align:middle
Sure. VICTORIA GUIDA.

38:33.746 --> 38:35.756 align:middle
You said that you're
still encouraging your,

38:36.116 --> 38:38.346 align:middle
your fellow Committee
members to submit forecasts,

38:38.346 --> 38:39.636 align:middle
even if you're not doing it.

38:39.826 --> 38:40.836 align:middle
So what do you think
is the benefit

38:40.836 --> 38:42.056 align:middle
of them doing it
even if you don't?

38:42.556 --> 38:43.126 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

38:43.126 --> 38:47.416 align:middle
That's the commitment that the
FOMC made, and it's a commitment

38:47.416 --> 38:49.076 align:middle
that I hope we live up to.

38:49.776 --> 38:52.546 align:middle
Commitment we made was to
deliver price stability.

38:52.676 --> 38:53.986 align:middle
I expect us to live up to it.

38:54.726 --> 38:58.776 align:middle
By the time we get to the end
of this year, as I mentioned,

38:58.776 --> 39:02.636 align:middle
I wouldn't be surprised if
there was a new communications

39:02.636 --> 39:05.386 align:middle
framework, there were
some changes to the SEP.

39:05.566 --> 39:08.226 align:middle
That's a Committee
discussion, a robust discussion,

39:08.226 --> 39:08.936 align:middle
and I think we'll have it.

39:09.456 --> 39:13.406 align:middle
I believe we're going to come to
a better mix of communications

39:13.406 --> 39:18.606 align:middle
to deliver on what we
promised, but I wouldn't want

39:18.606 --> 39:19.896 align:middle
to prejudge what those are.

39:19.956 --> 39:22.006 align:middle
But between now and
then, I would continue

39:22.006 --> 39:24.536 align:middle
to expect colleagues
to submit their SEPs.

39:24.996 --> 39:29.776 align:middle
Some of them I think
believe that the practice

39:29.776 --> 39:31.736 align:middle
as currently structured is okay.

39:32.176 --> 39:34.446 align:middle
But I heard a lot of
interest in real reform,

39:34.446 --> 39:36.186 align:middle
generally about all
of these topics.

39:36.816 --> 39:38.816 align:middle
You didn't ask it,
but I'll answer.

39:39.246 --> 39:42.016 align:middle
It was a pretty gracious
couple of days,

39:42.016 --> 39:46.746 align:middle
and it's been a pretty
warm few weeks.

39:46.746 --> 39:50.796 align:middle
The institution wants to figure
out how we can do better.

39:51.056 --> 39:53.596 align:middle
The institution's going
back to first principles.

39:54.056 --> 39:56.666 align:middle
And I'm encouraged that what
we've done in the statement,

39:56.666 --> 39:59.346 align:middle
what we're thinking about
doing with respect to the SEP,

39:59.666 --> 40:02.856 align:middle
that instinct towards a
new chapter is a real one.

40:02.856 --> 40:05.416 align:middle
And by the end of the year,
I hope we can put some points

40:05.416 --> 40:09.416 align:middle
on the board both in form and
in substance of delivering.

40:09.616 --> 40:10.956 align:middle
MICHELLE SMITH.

40:11.156 --> 40:12.476 align:middle
Go to Enda for the
last question.

40:13.246 --> 40:13.616 align:middle
ENDA CURRAN.

40:14.056 --> 40:15.636 align:middle
Thank you, Mr. Chairman.

40:15.636 --> 40:16.426 align:middle
Enda Curran, Bloomberg News.

40:16.836 --> 40:19.626 align:middle
Could you guide us through,
please, some of the principles

40:19.936 --> 40:21.956 align:middle
that guide your own
reaction function,

40:22.406 --> 40:24.436 align:middle
and tell us a little bit
about kind of conditions

40:24.476 --> 40:26.696 align:middle
that you think when
the Fed should respond.

40:26.696 --> 40:26.996 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

40:27.556 --> 40:31.586 align:middle
It's going to be a very
unsatisfactory answer

40:31.586 --> 40:32.866 align:middle
to the final question.

40:34.506 --> 40:38.986 align:middle
The Federal Reserve has a
lot of responsibilities,

40:38.986 --> 40:42.376 align:middle
not just in monetary policy, but
in supervision and regulation,

40:42.496 --> 40:44.406 align:middle
consumer affairs, and payments.

40:45.386 --> 40:49.506 align:middle
My own view is our credibility
comes from delivering

40:49.506 --> 40:52.426 align:middle
on what we're saying we're going
to do across everything we do.

40:52.426 --> 40:56.546 align:middle
I've devoted more time
in my first three weeks

40:56.546 --> 40:59.066 align:middle
to monetary policy
than all those things.

40:59.656 --> 41:02.646 align:middle
But the more we deliver on our
promises as good supervisors

41:02.646 --> 41:05.016 align:middle
and good regulators,
the more benefit we get,

41:05.016 --> 41:07.956 align:middle
the more credibility enhancement
we have in monetary policy.

41:08.696 --> 41:12.086 align:middle
When we deliver on our
price-stability objectives,

41:12.146 --> 41:16.056 align:middle
which we will, the
American people will feel

41:16.056 --> 41:18.726 align:middle
as though the hardships that
they've been living through,

41:18.726 --> 41:20.946 align:middle
in part because of inflation
in the last five years,

41:20.946 --> 41:22.146 align:middle
are in the rearview mirror,

41:22.626 --> 41:24.566 align:middle
and that credibility
will have dividends,

41:24.566 --> 41:26.556 align:middle
dividends across what we do.

41:26.556 --> 41:28.886 align:middle
And the institution will
come to press conferences

41:28.886 --> 41:31.986 align:middle
like this always with
an impetus to reform,

41:31.986 --> 41:34.726 align:middle
always with an impetus
to, to do better.

41:35.236 --> 41:36.586 align:middle
But we're going to put
some points on the board.

41:37.066 --> 41:37.796 align:middle
ENDA CURRAN.

41:37.796 --> 41:40.016 align:middle
Can I ask you about the
labor data in recent months?

41:40.016 --> 41:42.556 align:middle
How would you sum up labor
data-labor market right now,

41:42.556 --> 41:43.516 align:middle
do you see it as stable,

41:43.566 --> 41:45.446 align:middle
or as potentially a
source of inflation?

41:45.606 --> 41:45.866 align:middle
Thank you.

41:46.016 --> 41:46.316 align:middle
CHAIRMAN WARSH.

41:46.316 --> 41:50.986 align:middle
Yeah. So, so the
Committee-if I were to try

41:50.986 --> 41:53.296 align:middle
to capture how the
Committee thought about it,

41:53.296 --> 41:55.926 align:middle
the Committee thought that
the labor markets were stable.

41:56.786 --> 42:00.236 align:middle
There were some people around
the Committee who thought

42:00.236 --> 42:03.306 align:middle
that it was trending
better than that.

42:03.736 --> 42:06.276 align:middle
Trends matter more
than data points.

42:06.766 --> 42:10.376 align:middle
What's happening over three
or six months matters more

42:10.376 --> 42:13.096 align:middle
than any one data
point-any one data release.

42:13.666 --> 42:16.856 align:middle
And I'd say the jobs data's
been moving in a good direction.

42:17.666 --> 42:20.366 align:middle
If I heard one other
thing around that subject

42:20.366 --> 42:24.486 align:middle
over the course of the last
couple of days, what I heard was

42:24.536 --> 42:30.216 align:middle
that strong productivity-led
growth is not something

42:30.216 --> 42:32.156 align:middle
that we fear, but
something we embrace.

42:32.496 --> 42:37.246 align:middle
Thank you all very much.

